#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "f8b96980-910c-5e33-b6bf-e1f8532dcdbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10060" :question/problem "Given that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(2,3^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 6,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(10\\leq Y\\leq 14),$ where  $Y = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{6}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6026 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6064 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6103 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6141 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6406 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6443 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6480 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6808 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6844 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6879 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(\\mu,\\sigma^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_n\\sim N(n\\mu, \\: n\\sigma^2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{6}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{6} & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot {3}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(6\\cdot 2,\\,6\\cdot 9) \\\\  & =N(12,54).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(10\\leq Y \\leq 14).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10\\le Y\\le 14) & =P(\\frac{10-12}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{14-12}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{54}}) \\\\  & =P(-0.27\\le Z\\le 0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-Φ(-0.27) \\\\  & =Φ(0.27)-(1-Φ(0.27)) \\\\  & =2Φ(0.27)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.27) = 0.6064.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(10\\leq Y \\leq 14) = 2\\Phi(0.27) - 1 = 0.2128.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188213 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/id #uuid "c1eefedf-bc2e-5c6c-84e8-0c80aee4a4cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-9267" :question/problem "In a library, each shelf is filled with $20$ volumes of an encyclopedia, plus one bookend. The weight of one volume, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $150\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $5\\,\\textrm g,$ while the weight of a bookend, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\textrm g$ and a standard deviation of $2\\,\\textrm g.$ If one shelf is randomly selected, what is the probability that it supports a weight of more than $3\\,000\\,\\textrm g?$ You may assume that the weights of the volumes and bookend are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 2.0 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9772 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9778 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9783 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9788 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.1 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9821 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9826 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9830 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9834 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 2.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9861 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9864 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9868 \\,$ | $\\, 0.9871 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X_i$ denote the weight of the $i$th volume, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a bookend. Then, we have\n\n$$\nX_i \\sim N(150, 5^2), \\qquad 1\\leq i \\leq 20,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(50, 2^2).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected shelf. Then, since\n\n$$\nW = X_1+X_2+\\cdots + X_{20} + Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =20\\cdot 150+50 \\\\  & =3\\,050, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =20\\cdot {5}^{2}+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =504.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW \\sim N(3\\,050, 504).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W > 3\\,000),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & =P(Z>\\frac{3\\,000-3\\,050}{\\sqrt{504}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-2.23) \\\\  & =P(Z<2.23) \\\\  & =Φ(2.23)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(2.23)\\approx 0.9871.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W>3\\,000) & ≈Φ(2.23) \\\\  & =0.9871.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5f4694c-984e-560f-9bf5-4d005a4ac754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9051" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185527} {:answer/id #uuid "23500c3c-24f0-5446-b17f-acf2103a8562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185528} {:answer/id #uuid "32671e29-21ff-5171-bada-a3290f69c8fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9276" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185529} {:answer/id #uuid "506b2362-0b38-5291-bfc0-3ba673c907bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9151" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185530} {:answer/id #uuid "55006ef9-22df-5fdc-8f25-1e174ea1a758" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9251" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185531}] :answer-field/id #uuid "027be15c-3d8e-5694-8ce5-a754e8251921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185526}] :question/id #uuid "76bca3b1-738a-54b2-9eae-c2a2be05e5fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114465" :question/problem "In a particular shop, eggs are sold in cartons. There are $10$ eggs in each carton. The weight of an egg, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $60g$ and a standard deviation of $5g$, while the weight of a carton, in grams, is normally distributed with a mean of $30g$ and a standard deviation of $2g$. If one carton containing eggs is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $653g$? You may assume that the weights are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.00$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ | $0.04$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.2$ | $0.8849$ | $0.8869$ | $0.8888$ | $0.8907$ | $0.8925$ |\n| $1.3$ | $0.9032$ | $0.9049$ | $0.9066$ | $0.9082$ | $0.9099$ |\n| $1.4$ | $0.9192$ | $0.9207$ | $0.9222$ | $0.9236$ | $0.9251$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416644 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8fe2b95-0c68-58f7-a87a-5e4ad3d18e26" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "020bc723-6af1-5316-aaf4-e9de001b448a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7422" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416618} {:answer/id #uuid "0b1b048e-b282-560b-8713-335a9004952e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416619} {:answer/id #uuid "693ff683-c441-5136-b7ec-abf843bb1296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2877" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416627} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd73452-7201-5d58-8fb9-e272ec4a7b94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7329" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416634} {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7c85afb-f62b-5c4e-8deb-61eca859abde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2578" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416636} :answer-field/id #uuid "44ef41a7-faaf-5672-935c-ff24abe14152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c95c5475-5100-54a8-b160-4567ec570e4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143339" :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(0,{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 15,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\ge 1),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ | $\\,0.7123\\,$ | $\\,0.7157\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ | $\\,0.7454\\,$ | $\\,0.7486\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ | $\\,0.7764\\,$ | $\\,0.7794\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{15} & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(15\\cdot 0,\\,15\\cdot \\frac{4}{25}) \\\\  & =N(0,\\frac{12}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\ge 1).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{1-0}{\\sqrt{\\frac{12}{5}}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{12}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.65) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.65) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.65)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.65)=0.7422.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 1)=1-Φ(0.65)=0.2578.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d240e02-c926-5dcc-9494-315fdd662109" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Distributions and Probabilities With I.I.D Normal Random Variables In Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "815d9cd9-9ce6-5961-8d64-1767bb300297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7925" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185653} {:answer/id #uuid "9a422df5-ab92-54dd-a1c9-a0ceb47f6704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8822" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185654} {:answer/id #uuid "07bff180-83ad-5844-9ee6-3067e2a819f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185655} {:answer/id #uuid "178a9467-6b72-523c-8a18-5b4ace800e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8030" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185656} {:answer/id #uuid "32275441-e4de-5c6e-8e96-4fa22a6786a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185657}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4e3f6c50-ba8d-5d42-8b02-e27814486ca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185652}] :question/id #uuid "531df64f-e13b-5ec0-9ccc-69d48ab88b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-143362" :question/problem "A math test is composed of $15$ exercises. The time that a student takes to solve an exercise, in minutes, is normally distributed with a mean of $6min$ and a standard deviation of $2min$. If one student is randomly selected, what is the probability that they will take between $80min$ and $100min$ to complete all exercises? You may assume that the times spent solving each exercise are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $0.06$ | $0.07$ | $0.08$ | $0.09$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1.0$ | $0.8554$ | $0.8577$ | $0.8599$ | $0.8621$ |\n| $1.1$ | $0.8770$ | $0.8790$ | $0.8810$ | $0.8830$ |\n| $1.2$ | $0.8962$ | $0.8980$ | $0.8997$ | $0.9015$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
